THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-16) — exempt the platform service user from the cap
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Changed: App\Policies\WorkspacePolicy::create() now returns true for the
platform service user (config `relaticle.platform.service_user_email`) before
applying the per-user owned-workspace cap.

Reason: App\Actions\Platform\ProvisionWorkspace creates EVERY platform-provisioned
workspace owned by that one service user, and it delegates to upstream's own
CreateWorkspace, which authorises `create` through this policy. The upstream cap
(`relaticle.workspaces.max_owned_per_user`, default 10) is an anti-abuse limit for
HUMAN owners; applied to the single non-human provisioning identity it silently
capped the whole platform at 10 tenants, so the orchestrator's FATAL relaticle
provisioning step 403'd ("This action is unauthorized.") from tenant #11 on.

Files modified:
  app/Policies/WorkspacePolicy.php  `create()` exempts the service user; a private
                                    `isPlatformServiceUser()` matches the email
                                    against the `platform.service_user_email`
                                    config value. The cap still applies to every
                                    other user unchanged.

THIS IS THE FIRST FORK COMMIT THAT ALTERS AN UPSTREAM CODE PATH (the earlier
additions were new files only). The change is deliberately minimal and localised
to one method so it stays cheap to rebase; `main` is unaffected.


THE MODIFICATION (2026-10-21) — tenant-owned workspaces + a session broker
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Added: (a) a platform-guarded endpoint that mints a SINGLE-USE, short-lived
sign-in for a workspace's own owner, and (b) a public web route that redeems it
and establishes the panel session.

Reason: a brand that signed up on the platform console was still asked to log in
to the CRM — a second sign-up. The console brokers a module session from the
console login, but Relaticle's panel is session-based (Filament) and no Sanctum
bearer can stand in for a browser session. The hand-off is therefore a one-time
token the browser redeems, which is the only HTTP-only way in.

The same change gives a workspace an identity of its own. The first cut created
EVERY workspace under the shared platform service user, so no tenant had a
credential to broker AS, and all tenants shared one owner row. The owner is now
the tenant-scoped identity derived from the signup (`admin@<slug>.<base domain>`
— never a human's signup email, which is globally unique).

